# agent-browser

Fast browser automation CLI for AI agents. Chrome/Chromium via CDP with accessibility-tree snapshots and compact `@eN` element refs.

Install: `npm i -g agent-browser && agent-browser install`

## Local activation and native-tool precedence

This is an optional browser-verification capability, not a development methodology or an exhaustive test gate after every change. Prefer a functional native/equivalent interactive browser capability before this CLI. Inside Antigravity, use the built-in Browser Subagent/Chrome DevTools; Agent Browser must not displace it. On hosts without an equivalent tool, use this skill for relevant browser tasks automatically.

The CLI is already installed globally. Use its detected supported Chrome; download a runtime only if needed. Do not install Playwright CLI/MCP, Chrome DevTools MCP, or register Agent Browser MCP merely because the runtime-served guide documents them.

Launch on demand with a unique task-and-harness session name, passing `--session <unique-name>` on every browser command. Do not use the shared default session. Explicit flags work across PowerShell and POSIX shells; adapt the guide's Bash examples to the host shell. For a short-lived local task, pass `--idle-timeout 5m` consistently on every command, including close, as a cleanup backstop. Changing or omitting daemon-owned flags between commands can restart the daemon and discard transient page state.

Use a fresh isolated browser context by default. Attach to an existing browser or reuse/persist authenticated profiles/state only when relevant and authorized. Close only the session you own with `agent-browser --session <unique-name> close`, including after failures. Confirm it is absent from `agent-browser session list`. Do not run `close --all` when other agents may be active, or start a browser, dashboard, MCP, or daemon at system startup. The daemon may persist between commands within your active task; it must not be left running after the task.

These local scope and lifecycle rules govern the runtime-served examples below.

## Windows captured-output compatibility

On Windows, a freshly spawned daemon can retain captured pipe handles after the foreground CLI exits. For a complete known smoke-test flow, prefer the upstream `batch` command with `close` as the final command, so all steps run in one invocation and the output stream closes. Leave batch's default continue-on-error behavior enabled so cleanup is still attempted. Use string-command arguments or the documented stdin format, adapting quoting to the host shell. For incremental flows, capture the foreground CLI to real log files and wait for its PID rather than waiting for inherited pipe EOF; use the host's existing shell/process facilities, not a new wrapper, plugin, or MCP. Close your named session even after a failed check.

```text
agent-browser --session harness-task-unique --idle-timeout 5m batch "open https://example.com" "snapshot -i" "close"
```

